, measured deployment.</p>
<hr />
<h2 id="chapter-12-from-cnn-experiments-to-a-paper">Chapter 12 — From CNN Experiments to a Paper</h2>
<h3 id="what-reviewers-actually-check">What reviewers actually check</h3>
<p>A vision paper's credibility rests on a short list: <strong>(1)</strong> baselines that were actually tuned, <strong>(2)</strong> ablations showing each component earns its place, <strong>(3)</strong> statistics (seeds, variance), <strong>(4)</strong> a methods section detailed enough to reproduce, and <strong>(5)</strong> honest limitations. Everything in this chapter serves that list.</p>
<h3 id="ablations-proving-each-piece-matters">Ablations: proving each piece matters</h3>
<p>An <strong>ablation study</strong> removes (or swaps) one component at a time and measures the damage. It's how you answer "was it the architecture, the augmentation, or the pretraining?" Design ablations <em>before</em> running them — decide the question each row answers:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Variant</th>
<th>Accuracy (%)</th>
<th>Δ vs full</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Full model (EfficientNet-B0, fine-tuned, aug)</td>
<td>87.3 ± 0.4</td>
<td>—</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>− pretraining (from scratch)</td>
<td>81.1 ± 0.7</td>
<td>−6.2</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>− augmentation (no aug)</td>
<td>84.0 ± 0.5</td>
<td>−3.3</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>− fine-tuning (frozen backbone)</td>
<td>85.6 ± 0.3</td>
<td>−1.7</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Backbone → ResNet-50</td>
<td>86.9 ± 0.5</td>
<td>−0.4</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Each row isolates one decision. The Δ column is the story: pretraining mattered most, augmentation second. Rules: change exactly one thing per row; keep everything else (seeds, schedule, epochs) fixed; run 3+ seeds per row. Yes, ablations multiply compute — budget for them from the start. A paper with no ablations reads as "we tried things until a number went up," which is exactly what reviewers suspect.</p>
<h3 id="tables-the-results-sections-backbone">Tables: the results section's backbone</h3>
<p>Main results table conventions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rows</strong>: methods (yours + baselines), grouped logically. Baselines first, yours last (or bolded).</li>
<li><strong>Columns</strong>: metrics appropriate to the task (accuracy, F1, AUC — never accuracy alone under imbalance), plus params and FLOPs when efficiency is claimed.</li>
<li><strong>Every number</strong>: mean ± std over ≥3 seeds. A bare "87.3" with no variance is an anecdote.</li>
<li><strong>Bold</strong> the best per column; underline second-best (common convention).</li>
<li><strong>Footnotes</strong>: mark TTA, ensembling, or any inference-time trickery. "† uses 5-crop TTA."</li>
</ul>
<pre><code class="language-markdown">| Method | Backbone | Params | F1 (%) | AUC |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Baseline (frozen) | ResNet-50 | 23.5M | 78.2 ± 0.6 | 0.891 ± 0.004 |
| Fine-tuned | ResNet-50 | 23.5M | 83.4 ± 0.4 | 0.923 ± 0.003 |
| Ours | EfficientNet-B3 | 12.2M | **85.1 ± 0.3** | **0.936 ± 0.002** |
</code></pre>
<h3 id="reporting-checklist-methods-section">Reporting checklist (methods section)</h3>
<p>A reproducible methods section states: dataset (source, size, split ratios, split <em>unit</em> — patient/field/region), preprocessing (resize, normalization values), architecture (backbone, weight source + version, head design), training (optimizer, LR, schedule, epochs, batch size, augmentation policy with parameters, regularization, seeds, hardware, training time), evaluation (metrics, TTA if used, statistical treatment). If any item is missing, a reviewer can't reproduce your work — and will say so.</p>
<h3 id="figures-that-strengthen-the-paper">Figures that strengthen the paper</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Architecture diagram</strong>: one clean figure of your model (boxes + tensor shapes at each stage). Readers decide whether to keep reading based on this figure.</li>
<li><strong>Training curves</strong>: train/val loss + metric, showing convergence and the absence of overfitting drama.</li>
<li><strong>Qualitative results</strong>: best/worst Grad-CAM or segmentation overlays — including a failure case with a one-sentence hypothesis ("fails on low-contrast lesions; future work: contrast-aware augmentation").</li>
<li><strong>Ablation/confusion visuals</strong>: confusion matrix for classification; it shows <em>which</em> classes confuse the model, which is often the most interesting result.</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="limitations-and-ethics-write-them-before-the-camera-ready">Limitations and ethics (write them before the camera-ready)</h3>
<p>Every paper needs an honest limitations paragraph: dataset biases (single hospital, single season, single geography), failure modes you observed, compute constraints that limited ablations, and the gap between benchmark and deployment. For human-impact domains (medical, agriculture-as-livelihood), add the ethical note: intended use, who could be harmed by errors, and why the system is assistive rather than autonomous. Reviewers — and increasingly, conferences — expect this. It also happens to be good science.</p>
<h3 id="the-one-week-paper-sprint-a-realistic-plan">The one-week paper sprint (a realistic plan)</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Days 1–2</strong>: final baselines + ablations (seeds running in parallel).</li>
<li><strong>Day 3</strong>: figures (architecture, curves, Grad-CAM, confusion matrix).</li>
<li><strong>Day 4</strong>: results + methods draft (tables first, prose follows the tables).</li>
<li><strong>Day 5</strong>: intro + related work (related work is easier once you know exactly what your contribution is).</li>
<li><strong>Day 6</strong>: limitations, abstract, full read-through; check every number against logs.</li>
<li><strong>Day 7</strong>: co-author review buffer.</li>
</ul>
<p>The golden rule: <strong>tables before prose</strong>. Write the results tables first; the story they tell <em>is</em> your paper. Prose written before the numbers exist always needs rewriting.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>For your research.</strong> Start a "paper ledger" text file on day one of any project: every experiment gets one line (date, config, seed, result, one-line interpretation). When it's time to write, the ledger becomes your ablations and tables with zero archaeology. And version your datasets — a results table is meaningless if you can't say exactly which images were in the test split six months later. These two habits (ledger + dataset versioning) separate researchers whose work reproduces from those whose work doesn't.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3 id="statistics-code-release-and-surviving-peer-review">Statistics, code release, and surviving peer review</h3>
<p><strong>Beyond mean ± std: is the difference real?</strong> With three seeds each, "85.1 ± 0.3 vs 84.6 ± 0.4" might be noise. The cheap rigorous options: (a) <strong>paired bootstrap</strong> — resample the test set 1,000 times, compute the metric difference each time, and report the 95% confidence interval of the difference; if it excludes zero, claim the win; (b) more seeds (5–10) when compute allows — variance shrinks as 1/√n.</p>
<pre><code class="language-python">import numpy as np
rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
diffs = []
for _ in range(1000):
    idx = rng.integers(0, len(y_true), len(y_true))
    diffs.append(metric(y_true[idx], pred_a[idx]) - metric(y_true[idx], pred_b[idx]))
lo, hi = np.percentile(diffs, [2.5, 97.5])
print(f&quot;Δ = {np.mean(diffs):.3f}, 95% CI [{lo:.3f}, {hi:.3f}]&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>If the interval includes zero, write "no significant difference" — honesty here is what separates research from marketing, and reviewers can tell which one they're reading.</p>
<p><strong>Code and weight release checklist.</strong> "Code will be released" with no link increasingly irritates reviewers. Release at submission (anonymized) or at camera-ready at the latest: the training script, exact configs and hyperparameters, the environment file, pretrained weights, the figure-generation notebook, and a README that reproduces the main table in the stated hardware and time. Scrub hardcoded paths and credentials first. Your future self — reproducing this work for the journal extension — will thank you even more than the reviewers will.</p>
<p><strong>Responding to reviewers.</strong> The response letter is a genre with conventions: numbered responses mirroring the reviewers, each opening with what you <em>did</em> ("We added the requested ResNet-101 baseline; see Table 3, row 4"), quoting changed text where relevant, and conceding gracefully where the reviewer is right ("We agree the claim was overstated; we softened it on p. 4"). Never try to argue a reviewer into submission — persuade with new experiments instead. The most common revision requests for CNN papers are stronger baselines, ablations, variance reporting, and dataset details — exactly what this chapter told you to prepare <em>before</em> submission. A paper written to this chapter's checklist tends to get "accept with minor revisions" instead of "reject: insufficient evaluation."</p>
<p><strong>Venue strategy, in one paragraph.</strong> Workshops (fast, short papers) are for early results and feedback; conferences (CVPR/ICCV/ECCV, MICCAI for medical, or top domain venues) are for archival impact — note that the rebuttal phase rewards precisely the extra experiments this book teaches you to run; journals (TPAMI, Medical Image Analysis) are for extended, mature work with full ablations. Match the venue to the result's maturity, not to your impatience: a premature conference submission that gets rejected costs more time than a workshop paper that earns useful feedback.</p>
<h3 id="writing-related-work-the-abstract-and-a-reproducibility-checklist">Writing related work, the abstract, and a reproducibility checklist</h3>
<p><strong>Related work as positioning, not listing.</strong> The failure mode: a chronological dump of every CNN paper since 2012. The fix: organize by <em>the question your paper answers</em>. "Prior work improves accuracy via (a) architectures, (b) pretraining, (c) augmentation. We show that on [domain], (b) dominates and (a) is saturated — contrary to [cite], who found...". Each paragraph ends with the gap your work fills. Cite the originals (this book's references give you the canonical twelve), not the survey that cited them — reviewers check. And cite competitors fairly: the paper you're beating is doing you the favor of existing; misrepresenting it is the fastest way to get a hostile review from its authors.</p>
<p><strong>The abstract formula.</strong> Five sentences: (1) the problem and why it matters (one clause of domain motivation); (2) what prior work misses; (3) what you did (method, one sentence); (4) the key result with numbers ("92.1 ± 0.3% F1, +3.4 over the strongest baseline"); (5) the implication or released artifact. Write it last, even though it appears first — it's a summary of the tables, and the tables come first (Chapter 12's golden rule).</p>
<p><strong>Reproducibility checklist (paste into your repo's README).</strong>
- [ ] Dataset: source, version/hash, exact split files or split code with seed
- [ ] Preprocessing: resize, normalization values, channel handling
- [ ] Architecture: backbone name, weight source + version, head definition
- [ ] Training: optimizer, LR, schedule, epochs, batch size (effective, incl. accumulation), augmentation policy with parameters, regularization, seeds
- [ ] Hardware + software: GPU type, CUDA/PyTorch/timm versions, training time per run
- [ ] Evaluation: metrics, TTA policy if used, statistical treatment (seeds, CI)
- [ ] Checkpoints: final weights + best-validation weights, with config to reload them
- [ ] Figures: script/notebook regenerating every figure from logged outputs</p>
<p>If a stranger with your repo and a GPU can't reproduce your main table in the stated time, the checklist found the gap before the reviewers did. This is the unglamorous work that separates papers people build on from papers people cite and ignore.</p>
<h3 id="reviewer-comments-youll-get-and-the-fixes">Reviewer comments you'll get (and the fixes)</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Comment</th>
<th>What they mean</th>
<th>Fix</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>"Baselines are weak"</td>
<td>Your method beats an untuned strawman</td>
<td>Tune baseline LR/schedule; add a standard strong baseline (ResNet-50/EfficientNet)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"No ablation"</td>
<td>Can't tell what causes the gain</td>
<td>One-variable-per-row table (this chapter); budget compute upfront</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"Single seed"</td>
<td>Result might be luck</td>
<td>3+ seeds, mean ± std; bootstrap CI for close calls</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"Dataset details missing"</td>
<td>Can't judge or reproduce</td>
<td>Split unit, sizes, annotator agreement, preprocessing — full checklist</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"Incremental"</td>
<td>Contribution unclear</td>
<td>Sharpen the claim: one sentence on what we now know; lead with the ablation that proves it</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"Why this architecture?"</td>
<td>Choice unjustified</td>
<td>RF/param/FLOP reasoning (Chapters 1–3) or a 2-rung comparison</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"Overclaims"</td>
<td>Text exceeds evidence</td>
<td>Soften verbs (suggests→demonstrates only with stats); add limitations paragraph</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"No failure analysis"</td>
<td>Only successes shown</td>
<td>Confusion matrix + Grad-CAM failure case + one-sentence hypothesis each</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"Code?"</td>
<td>Reproducibility doubt</td>
<td>Release repo + weights + figure scripts at submission</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"Related work incomplete"</td>
<td>Missed key prior art</td>
<td>Position by idea-lineage (Chapter 4's timeline); cite originals, not surveys</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Keep this table next to the manuscript while drafting. Every row is a rejection reason you can eliminate <em>before</em> submission — which is the entire thesis of this chapter: papers are accepted in the months before writing, through the experiments you chose to run.</p>
<h3 id="failure-mode-gallery-start-yours-on-day-one">Failure-mode gallery (start yours on day one)</h3>
<p>Keep a running document of every informative failure with the image, prediction, Grad-CAM, and your hypothesis: (a) low-contrast lesions missed — hypothesis: augmentation never simulated them; fix: contrast jitter; (b) distant-field wheat classified as barley — hypothesis: texture-only decision (receptive field too small for head morphology); fix: larger input resolution; (c) night-time satellite tiles misclassified — hypothesis: no night examples in training; fix: collect or synthesize. Three columns — observation, hypothesis, fix — reviewed weekly. Half of these become ablation rows; one or two become the paper's limitations section; the gallery itself becomes your expertise. Researchers who keep failure galleries debug in days what takes others weeks.</p>
<p><strong>Key takeaways</strong>
- Reviewers check: tuned baselines, ablations, seed statistics, reproducible methods, honest limitations.
- Ablations change one thing per row, fixed everything else, 3+ seeds. Budget compute for them upfront.
- Tables: baselines then yours, mean ± std always, params/FLOPs when relevant, footnotes for TTA/ensembles.
- Methods must state: data, splits, preprocessing, architecture + weights, training recipe, evaluation, hardware.
- Tables before prose. Keep a paper ledger from day one. Write limitations honestly — it's good science and good strategy.</p>
