<h2 id="glossary">Glossary</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Activation map / feature map</strong>: The 2-D output grid produced by applying one kernel across the input; a layer's output volume stacks one map per kernel.</li>
<li><strong>Batch normalization</strong>: Layer that normalizes each channel to zero mean/unit variance per mini-batch (with learned scale/shift); stabilizes and accelerates training.</li>
<li><strong>Bottleneck block</strong>: Residual block using 1×1 → 3×3 → 1×1 convolutions to cut computation in deep ResNets.</li>
<li><strong>Channel</strong>: One 2-D slice of an image or feature volume (e.g., R/G/B); convolutions mix information across channels.</li>
<li><strong>Compound scaling</strong>: EfficientNet's rule for jointly scaling network depth, width, and input resolution.</li>
<li><strong>Convolution (deep learning)</strong>: Cross-correlation of a learned kernel over the input — sliding element-wise multiply-and-sum; the name "convolution" is conventional.</li>
<li><strong>Cross-correlation</strong>: The unflipped sliding-dot-product operation that deep learning libraries compute and call convolution.</li>
<li><strong>Data augmentation</strong>: Random transformations of training images (flips, crops, color shifts) that synthesize dataset variation.</li>
<li><strong>Depthwise separable convolution</strong>: Factorized convolution — a per-channel spatial filter followed by a 1×1 channel mixer; ~8–9× cheaper than standard convolution.</li>
<li><strong>Discriminative learning rates</strong>: Using smaller learning rates for early (pretrained) layers and larger ones for late layers/head during fine-tuning.</li>
<li><strong>Dropout</strong>: Regularization that randomly zeroes activations during training to prevent co-adaptation.</li>
<li><strong>Effective receptive field</strong>: The input region that actually influences a neuron — smaller and Gaussian-shaped compared to the theoretical receptive field.</li>
<li><strong>Feature extraction (transfer)</strong>: Freezing a pretrained backbone and training only a new classifier head.</li>
<li><strong>Fine-tuning</strong>: Training some or all of a pretrained network's weights on a new task, usually with small learning rates.</li>
<li><strong>FLOPs</strong>: Floating-point operations; a hardware-independent measure of a model's computational cost.</li>
<li><strong>Global average pooling (GAP)</strong>: Averaging each feature map to a single value; the standard parameter-free bridge to the classifier.</li>
<li><strong>Grad-CAM</strong>: Visualization weighting a layer's feature maps by their gradient-derived importance for a class, showing influential image regions.</li>
<li><strong>Inductive bias</strong>: Assumptions built into an architecture (e.g., locality and weight sharing in CNNs) that guide learning.</li>
<li><strong>Kernel / filter</strong>: The small learned weight matrix slid across the input in a convolution.</li>
<li><strong>Label smoothing</strong>: Regularization that softens one-hot targets, discouraging overconfident predictions.</li>
<li><strong>Negative transfer</strong>: When pretraining on a source domain hurts (rather than helps) performance on the target domain.</li>
<li><strong>Padding</strong>: Border pixels added around an input so convolution preserves spatial size ("same" padding) or controls shrinkage.</li>
<li><strong>Pooling</strong>: Fixed downsampling operation (max/average) over windows; gives approximate translation invariance, no learned parameters.</li>
<li><strong>Receptive field</strong>: The input-image region that can affect a given neuron's value; grows with depth and stride.</li>
<li><strong>Residual / skip connection</strong>: An identity shortcut adding a block's input to its output (y = F(x) + x); enables very deep networks by preserving gradient flow.</li>
<li><strong>Stride</strong>: The step size of the sliding kernel; stride &gt; 1 downsamples the output.</li>
<li><strong>Test-time augmentation (TTA)</strong>: Averaging predictions over augmented versions of each test image for free accuracy gains.</li>
<li><strong>Translation equivariance</strong>: A shift of the input shifts the feature map equally (property of convolution).</li>
<li><strong>Translation invariance</strong>: A shift of the input leaves the output (approximately) unchanged (property of pooling).</li>
<li><strong>Warmup</strong>: Gradually ramping the learning rate from 0 at the start of training to avoid early instability.</li>
<li><strong>Weight decay</strong>: L2 penalty on weights; the baseline regularizer, decoupled from gradients in AdamW.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<ul>
<li><strong>Atrous/dilated convolution</strong>: Convolution with gaps inserted in the kernel, enlarging the receptive field without extra parameters or resolution loss.</li>
<li><strong>Backbone</strong>: The pretrained convolutional feature extractor (e.g., ResNet-50) to which a task-specific head is attached.</li>
<li><strong>Catastrophic forgetting</strong>: Loss of pretrained features during aggressive fine-tuning on a small dataset.</li>
<li><strong>Checkpoint</strong>: Saved model weights (and optimizer state) during/after training; best-validation checkpointing is standard.</li>
<li><strong>Concept activation vector (TCAV)</strong>: Direction in activation space representing a human-defined concept, used to quantify a model's reliance on it.</li>
<li><strong>Confusion matrix</strong>: Table of predicted vs. true classes showing exactly which classes a classifier confuses.</li>
<li><strong>CutMix</strong>: Augmentation pasting a patch from one image onto another, mixing labels by area proportion.</li>
<li><strong>Effective batch size</strong>: Batch size × gradient-accumulation steps; the batch size the optimizer effectively sees.</li>
<li><strong>Head</strong>: Task-specific layers (usually classifier) attached to a pretrained backbone.</li>
<li><strong>Learning-rate finder</strong>: Single-run exponential LR sweep used to pick a training LR (Smith, 2017).</li>
<li><strong>Linear probing</strong>: Training only the classifier head on frozen backbone features; first stage of LP-FT.</li>
<li><strong>MixUp</strong>: Augmentation blending two images and their labels with a random mixing coefficient.</li>
<li><strong>Quantization</strong>: Reducing weight/activation precision (e.g., FP32→INT8) to shrink models and speed inference.</li>
<li><strong>SyncBatchNorm</strong>: Batch normalization with statistics synchronized across GPUs in distributed training.</li>
<li><strong>Weight tying</strong>: Sharing the same parameters across positions or layers (convolution's core mechanism).</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="practice-exercises">Practice Exercises</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Hand convolution.</strong> Using the 5×5 input and 3×3 vertical-edge kernel from Chapter 2, compute the output at position (1, 1) by hand, then verify with the PyTorch snippet. Then recompute with stride 2 and padding 1, predicting the output shape first with the sizing formula.</li>
<li><strong>Parameter audit.</strong> For a network with: conv1 (3→64, 7×7, stride 2), maxpool, conv2 (64→128, 3×3), conv3 (128→256, 3×3), GAP, FC (256→10) — compute total parameters by hand using the per-layer formula, then verify in PyTorch. Which layer dominates the parameter count?</li>
<li><strong>Receptive field mapping.</strong> Compute the theoretical receptive field at each stage of the network in Exercise 2 (input 224×224). Is the final RF large enough to cover a 100-pixel object? What would you change if it weren't?</li>
<li><strong>Build a residual block from scratch.</strong> Implement <code>BasicBlock</code> (Chapter 5) without looking, then stack 8 of them into a tiny ResNet for CIFAR-10. Train it for 20 epochs. Then remove the skip connections and retrain — compare training loss curves and explain the difference.</li>
<li><strong>Research: ablation design.</strong> Pick a published CNN paper in your domain. List every design decision the authors made (backbone, augmentation, pretraining, loss). Design a 5-row ablation table that isolates the paper's central claim. Which row would falsify the claim if it showed no drop?</li>
<li><strong>Transfer learning shootout.</strong> Take a small public dataset in your domain (or CIFAR-10 as proxy). Compare three protocols with identical budgets: (a) from-scratch training, (b) frozen-backbone feature extraction, (c) staged fine-tuning with discriminative LRs. Report mean ± std over 3 seeds. Which wins, and by how much per protocol?</li>
<li><strong>Augmentation forensics.</strong> Build the training augmentation pipeline from Chapter 8, save a 64-image grid of augmented samples from <em>your</em> dataset, and inspect every image. Find one augmentation that violates label preservation for your data and justify its removal in two sentences (methods-section style).</li>
<li><strong>Research: spurious correlation hunt.</strong> Train a classifier, then run Grad-CAM on 50 test images. Find at least one case where the heatmap focuses outside the object of interest. Hypothesize the spurious cue, propose a data or augmentation fix, and re-run. Did the heatmap move?</li>
<li><strong>Efficiency under constraint.</strong> You have one GPU and 12 hours. Compare EfficientNet-B0, MobileNetV3-Large, and ResNet-18 (all pretrained, fine-tuned) on your task: report accuracy, params, FLOPs, and measured inference latency on your target device. Write the one-paragraph "model selection" justification for your paper.</li>
<li><strong>Research: from ledger to paper.</strong> Run a 4-experiment mini-project (baseline + 3 ablations, 3 seeds each) on any image task, keeping a paper ledger (date, config, seed, result, interpretation — Chapter 12). Produce: one results table with mean ± std, one training-curve figure, one Grad-CAM figure with an honest caption, and a limitations paragraph. This is a complete paper skeleton — keep the habit.</li>
</ol>
